'''top-minor''' (top′mī″nọr), ''n.'' In ''rope-making,'' one of the holes through which the individual strands are drawn on the way to the twisting-machine.

'''topmost''' (top′mōst), ''a. superl.'' [< ''top''<sup>1</sup> + ''-most.''] Highest; uppermost.

{{smaller block|Whose far-down pines are wont to tear<br>Locks of wool from the ''topmost'' cloud.
{{right|''Lowell,'' Appledore, ii.}}}}

'''topographer''' (tọ̄-pog′ra̤-fėr), ''n.'' [< ''topograph-y'' ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] One who describes a particular place, town, city, tract of land, or country; one who is skilled in topography.

{{smaller block|All the ''topographers'' that ever writ of . . . a town or countrey.
{{right|''Howell,'' Forreine Travell (ed. Arber), p. 12.}}}}

'''topographic''' (top-ọ̄-graf′ik), ''a.'' [= F. ''topographique'' = Sp. ''topográfico'' = Pg. ''topographico'' = It. ''topografico;'' as ''topograph-y'' + ''-ic.''] Same as ''topographical.''

{{smaller block|The ''topographic'' description of this mighty empire.
{{right|''Sir T. Herbert,'' Travels, p. 58.}}}}

'''Topographic chart.''' {{smaller|See ''chart.''}}

'''topographical''' (top-ọ̄-graf′i-ka̤l), ''a.'' [< ''topographic'' + ''-al.''] Of or pertaining to topography; of the nature of topography.—'''Topographical anatomy.''' {{smaller|See ''anatomy,'' and ''topography,'' 4.}}—'''Topographical surveying.''' {{smaller|See ''surveying.''}}

'''topographically''' (top-ọ̄-graf′i-ka̤l-i), ''adv.'' In the manner of topography. ''Fuller,'' Worthies,

