'''toponymy''' (tō-pon′i-mi), ''n.'' [< Gr. τόπος, a place, + ὄνομα, ὄνυμα, name.] In ''anat.'', the designation of the position and direction of parts and organs, as distinguished from the names of the parts and organs themselves, which is the province of organonymy; regional or topographical nomenclature; topical terminology.—{{smaller|'''Extrinsic toponymy,''' the use of descriptive terms based upon the attitude of an animal in relation to the earth, as anterior, posterior, vertical, horizontal, etc. See the quotation under ''superior'', ''a.'', 2.—'''Intrinsic toponymy,''' the use of terms referring to regions of the animal itself, regardless of its habitual posture, as dorsal, ventral, ental, ectal, etc.}}

'''topophone''' (top′ō-fōn), ''n.'' [< Gr. τόπος, a place, + φωνή, a sound, tone.] An instrument, invented by A. M. Mayer, for ascertaining the direction from which any sound proceeds, as the sound of a bell, whistle, or fog-horn at sea in thick weather. {{smaller|It consists essentially of a horizontal bar pivoted at the center so as to turn freely in any direction. At each end of the bar is a resonator opening in the same direction, each connected with a sound-tube for the corresponding ear of the observer. On moving the bar about, a position will be found in which both resonators face the source of the sound, when the sounds heard through the two tubes will be increased or reinforced. In any other position the sounds will be weakened. The direction of the sound when loudest will be at a right angle with the bar.}}

'''top-pendant''' (top′pen″dant), ''n.'' ''Naut.'', a large rope used in sending topmasts up or down.

'''topper''' (top′ėr), ''n.'' [< ''top''<sup>1</sup> + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] 1. One who or that which tops. {{smaller|(''a'') The upper part, layer, or covering of anything. [Colloq.]}}

readings.c2s4:
¶
{{smaller block|There was a boy beaten by a woman not long since for selling a big pottle of strawberries that was rubbish all under the ''toppers.'' It was all strawberry leaves, and crushed strawberries, and such like.
{{right|''Mayhew,'' London Labour and London Poor, II. 137.}}}}

{{smaller|(''b'') One who or that which excels; anything surpassing or extraordinary. [Colloq.]}} 2. A blow on the head. ''Hotten.'' [Slang.]—3. Same as ''float-file'' (which see, under ''file''<sup>1</sup>). ''E. H. Knight.''—4. The stump of a smoked cigar; the tobacco which is left in the bottom of a pipe-bowl. ''Encyc. Dict.''

'''toppice†''', ''v.'' Same as ''tappice'' for ''tappish.''

'''topping''' (top′ing), ''n.'' [< ME. ''toppyng;'' verbal n. of ''top''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.''] 1. The act of one who tops. {{smaller|(''a'') The act or practice of cutting off the top, as of a tree or plant.}}

{{smaller block|The pruning-knife—zounds!—the axe! Why, here has been such lopping and ''topping,'' I sha'n't have the bare trunk of my play left presently.
{{right|''Sheridan,'' The Critic, ii. 2.}}}}

readings.c3s1:
{{smaller|(''b'') ''Naut.'', the act of pulling one extremity of a yard or boom higher than the other. (''c'') The act of reducing to an exact level the points of the teeth of a saw.}}

2. That which tops; the upper part of anything; especially, a crest of hair, feathers, etc., upon the head: said of a forelock or topknot, an egret, the mane of a horse, etc.

{{smaller block|The mane of that mayn hors much to hit lyke, . . .<br>The tayl & his ''toppyng'' twynnen of a sute,<br>& bounden bothe wyth a bande of a bryȝt grene.
{{right|''Sir Gawayne and the Green Knight'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 191.}}}}

3. ''pl.'' That which is cut off in topping, as the branches of a tree.—4. ''pl.'' That which comes from hemp in the process of hatcheling.—5. The tail of an artificial fly, used by anglers, usually a feather from the crest of the golden pheasant. ''Sportsman's Gazetteer'', p. 599.

'''topping''' (top′ing), ''p. a.'' 1. Rising above all others; loftiest; overtopping.

{{smaller block|Ridges of lofty and ''topping'' mountains.
{{right|''Derham'', Physico-Theol. (''Latham.'')}}}}

2. Surpassing; towering; preëminent; distinguished.

{{smaller block|The thoughts of the mind . . . are uninterruptedly employed that way, by the determination of the will, influenced by that ''topping'' uneasiness as long as it lasts.
{{right|''Locke'', Human Understanding, II. xxi. § 38.}}}}

{{smaller block|I have heard say he [the Governor of Achin] had not less than 1000 Slaves, some of whom were ''topping'' Merchants, and had many Slaves under them.
{{right|''Dampier'', Voyages, II. i. 141.}}}}

{{smaller block|Of all who have attempted Homer, he [Chapman] has the ''topping'' merit of being inspired by him.
{{right|''Lowell'', Study Windows, p. 326.}}}}

3. Lofty; pretentious; assuming; arrogant.

readings.c3s2:
¶
{{smaller block|The Friend was a poor little man, of a low condition and mean appearance; whereas these two Baptists were ''topping'' blades, that looked high and spake big.
{{right|''T. Ellwood'', Life (ed. Howells), p. 291.}}}}

{{smaller block|I have a project of turning three or four of our most ''topping'' fellows into doggrel.
{{right|''Farquhar'', Love and a Bottle, iii. 2.}}}}

4. Fine; well; excellent. [Prov. Eng.]

{{smaller block|I don't like her to come by herself, now she 's not so terrible ''topping'' in health.
{{right|''T. Hardy'', Under the Greenwood Tree, iv. 4.}}}}

'''topping-lift''' (top′ing-lift), ''n.'' See ''lift''<sup>2</sup>.

'''toppingly''' (top′ing-li), ''a.'' [< ''topping'' + ''-ly''<sup>1</sup>.] 1†. Topping; fine.

{{smaller block|These ''toppingly'' guests be in number but ten,
As welcome in dairy as bears among men.
{{right|''Tusser'', April's Husbandry, Lesson for Dairy-Maid.}}}}

2. In good health; well. ''Halliwell.'' [Prov. Eng.]

'''toppingly†''' (top′ing-li), ''adv.'' [< ''topping'' + ''-ly''<sup>2</sup>.] In a topping manner; eminently; finely; roundly.

{{smaller block|I mean to marry her ''toppingly'' when she least thinks of it.
{{right|''Jarvis'', tr. of Don Quixote, II. iii. 18. (''Davies.'')}}}}

'''topple''' (top′l), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''toppled'', ppr. ''toppling''. [Freq. of ''top''<sup>1</sup>; possibly an accom. form of ME. ''torple'', q. v.] I. ''intrans.'' 1. To fall top or head foremost; fall forward as having too heavy a top; pitch or tumble down.

{{smaller block|Though castles ''topple'' on their warders' heads;
Though palaces and pyramids do slope
Their heads to their foundations.
{{right|''Shak.'', Macbeth, iv. 1. 56.}}}}
